{
  "id": "Compound::DB00536 - Gene::3756",
  "verbalization": "Guanidine exhibits a strong binding interaction with multiple potassium channel subunits, specifically Kcnv1, Kcns3, and Kcna3, all of which are involved in delayed rectifier potassium channel activity. This activity is ultimately linked to the function of Kcnh1, a key component of the delayed rectifier potassium channel. The consistent scores across the three paths suggest a robust and reliable interaction between Guanidine and Kcnh1, mediated through the shared involvement of these potassium channels. The mechanistic evidence indicates that Guanidine may modulate Kcnh1 function by influencing the activity of these channels, potentially through direct binding or by altering the overall channel activity. The presence of a well-documented pathway involving delayed rectifier potassium channels supports the hypothesis that Guanidine could exert a pharmacological effect on Kcnh1, though further experimental validation is required to confirm the exact mechanism and therapeutic relevance.",
